<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</h1><p class="st-meta">1699 CE – 1826 CE · capital Coburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ernestine duchy in Franconia and Thuringia whose ambitious family married into the royal houses of Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ernest-iv-duke-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">John Ernest</a>, seventh son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-gotha">Ernest the Pious</a>, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> in 1680. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-saxe-coburg">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg</a> died out in 1699, he gained most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> after a long dispute, forming Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was Lutheran and small, with debts that led to an imperial debt commission in the 18th century. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-frederick-duke-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">Ernest Frederick</a> and Duke Francis governed modestly; Francis was a collector whose prints formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> collections. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Ernest I</a> was restored after French occupation in 1807. Francis's children married well: Leopold later became King of the Belgians and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a>, Duchess of Kent, was mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg</a> died out, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Ernest I</a> gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> in 1826, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</a>. Saalfeld went to Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, and the family's dynastic rise continued under the new name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_Saxe-Saalfeld_(1680).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1699 CE – 1826 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Coburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Ernest, Francis, Ernest I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Coburg-Saalfeld</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 431 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5251 687L5244 667M5251 687L5229 694M5251 687L5199 691M5251 687L5251 687M5251 687L5167 706"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5244 667h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5251 687h0M5167 706h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Coburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
